module bindings.c.assert_only_length_copy;

// memcpy(3), memmove(3), strncpy(3) and strncat(3) copy exactly the count they
// are handed, so a function that bounds that count only inside assert() has
// written down the amount it depends on and made no runtime decision with it:
// assert() compiles to nothing in a build that defines NDEBUG, and the copy
// then takes whatever the caller passes. The comparison's presence is the
// reading -- the bound was meant to hold -- and its construct is the defect,
// because the same comparison in an if, a switch case or a loop condition
// does run. That separates this from a copy nobody compared, which is a much
// larger set and carries no statement of intent at all.
//
// The fact is emitted by the C frontend (ccAssertOnlyLengthCopyObservations,
// internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go) from the syntax tree, and is
// callee-neutral, so which spellings count as raw copies is decided here, in
// data: the four C standard byte-count copy primitives and documented library
// aliases of the same signature. The next project's prefixed spelling is a
// binding edit, not an engine change.
//
// Residuals, all false-negative: the frontend reads the comparison anywhere in
// the function, so a bound checked for a sibling copy clears this one too; the
// compacted body keeps comments, so a commented-out runtime check still clears;
// and a destination the function allocates by the count itself is bounded by
// construction, which the fact cannot see, so those sites are reported
// alongside the ones that cannot be.
binding assertOnlyLengthCopy {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.kind == "any" and node.analysis == "copy.assert_only_length_bound" and containsAny(node.token, ["callee=memcpy", "callee=memmove", "callee=strncpy", "callee=strncat", "callee=pj_memcpy", "callee=SDL_memcpy"])
  emit issue code.AssertOnlyLengthCopy at node
}
